<h2>Day 1: <a id=3D"Day1" name=3D"Day1">The Fundamental Units</a></h2>

<p>To understand this, one needs to know a bit about the metric system and =
dimensional equation. Please read <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Metric_system">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metric_system</a> and <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimensional_analysis">https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Dimensional_analysis</a>.</p>

<p>We use the 7 base units of the SI (<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/International_System_of_Units">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internatio=
nal_System_of_Units</a>) as a starting point: these are the <code>Fundament=
alMeasureUnit</code>. A fundamental unit is semantically equivalent (for us=
) to a dimension. We define 3 fundamental units (dimensions) and new ones c=
an be dynamically added to the system as required (for instance =E2=80=9C$=
=E2=80=9D and/or =E2=80=9C=E2=82=AC=E2=80=9D, =E2=80=9C=C2=A3=E2=80=9D, etc=
.). Note that each currency would be a dimension. Converting a quantity of =
=E2=80=9C$=E2=80=9D to =E2=80=9C=E2=82=AC=E2=80=9D is not in the scope of t=
his library, it must be done externally.</p>

<p>The three fundamental units we define by default are:</p>

