margin-top: 0px;">var metre =3D MeasureUnit.Metre;
var second =3D MeasureUnit.Second;
var kilogram =3D MeasureUnit.Kilogram;
var ampere =3D MeasureUnit.Ampere;
var candela =3D MeasureUnit.Candela;
var squaredMeter =3D metre^2;

var hertz =3D MeasureUnit.None / second;
hertz.Abbreviation.Should().Be( "s-1" );

var rad =3D metre * (metre ^ -1);
rad.Should().BeSameAs( MeasureUnit.None );

var steradian =3D squaredMeter / squaredMeter;
steradian.Should().BeSameAs( MeasureUnit.None );

var newton =3D kilogram * metre * (second ^ -2);
newton.Abbreviation.Should().Be( "kg.m.s-2" );

var pascal =3D newton / squaredMeter;
pascal.Abbreviation.Should().Be( "kg.m-1.s-2" );

var joule =3D newton * metre;
var watt =3D joule / second;
var coulomb =3D ampere * second;
var volt =3D watt / ampere;

// Another definition of the volt:
var volt2 =3D joule / coulomb;

volt.Should().BeSameAs( volt2 );
volt.Abbreviation.Should().Be( "m2.kg.A-1.s-3" );

var farad =3D coulomb / volt;
farad.Abbreviation.Should().Be( "s4.A2.kg-1.m-2" );

var ohm =3D volt / ampere;
ohm.Abbreviation.Should().Be( "m2.kg.A-2.s-3" );

// Another definition of the farad.
var farad2 =3D second / ohm;
farad2.Should().BeSameAs( farad );

var siemens =3D MeasureUnit.None / ohm;
var siemens2 =3D ampere / volt;
siemens.Should().BeSameAs( siemens2 );
siemens.Abbreviation.Should().Be( "s3.A2.kg-1.m-2" );

var weber =3D joule / ampere;
var tesla =3D volt * second / squaredMeter;
var tesla2 =3D weber / squaredMeter;
var tesla3 =3D newton / (ampere * metre);

tesla2.Should().BeSameAs( tesla );
tesla3.Should().BeSameAs( tesla );
tesla.Abbreviation.Should().Be( "kg.A-1.s-2" );

var henry =3D ohm * second;
var henry2 =3D volt * second / ampere;
var henry3 =3D weber / ampere;
henry2.Should().BeSameAs( henry );
henry3.Should().BeSameAs( henry );
henry.Abbreviation.Should().Be( "m2.kg.A-2.s-2" );

var lumen =3D candela * steradian;
var lux =3D lumen / squaredMeter;
lux.Abbreviation.Should().Be( "cd.m-2" );
</pre>

<p><strong>Important note</strong>: The code above shows that the objects a=
re actually the same object (reference equality) when they define the same =
unit of measure. This is one of the goals of this library: units must be fu=
lly normalized and cached even if new unit of measure can be dynamically cr=
eated at any time. This is achieved thanks to a <code>ConcurrentDictionary<=
/code>, the =E2=80=9C<code>Abbreviation</code>=E2=80=9D property of the mea=
sures being the key.</p>

<p>Even if it works like a charm and is funny, this is not enough. We must =
handle:</p>

<ul>
	<li><strong>Prefixes</strong>: Standard ones like <code>=E2=80=9Cd=E2=80=
=9D/=E2=80=9DDeci=E2=80=9D</code> like in <code>=E2=80=9Cdm=E2=80=9D/=E2=80=
=9DDecimeter=E2=80=9D</code> or <code>=E2=80=9Cm=E2=80=9D/=E2=80=9DMilli=E2=
=80=9D</code> like in <code>=E2=80=9Cmm=E2=80=9D/=E2=80=9DMillimeter=E2=80=
=9D</code>. See <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metric_prefix">htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metric_prefix</a> and since we added bit, we sho=
uld also handle binary prefixes (see <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Binary_prefix">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binary_prefix</a>): <code>=
=E2=80=9CKi=E2=80=9D/=E2=80=9DKibbi=E2=80=9D</code> like <code>=E2=80=9CKib=
=E2=80=9D/=E2=80=9DKibiBit=E2=80=9D</code>.

	<p>Introducing this is not as easy as it seems. We must be able to transpa=
rently handle equivalence like, for instance, the fact that m.s-2 (metre pe=
r squared second, i.e., an acceleration) is the same as =CE=BCm.ms-2 (micro=
meter per squared milliseconds).</p>
	</li>	<li><strong>Derived units</strong>: A Derived Unit is like an alias,=
 for instance <code>=E2=80=9Cl=E2=80=9D/=E2=80=9DLiter=E2=80=9D</code> is t=
he same as <code>=E2=80=9Cdm3=E2=80=9D</code>, i.e., 10-3 m3.<br>
	Some of the actually used units introduce a factor (that does not fit into=
 a simple exponentiation like the liter).
	<p>For instance:</p>

	<ul>
		<li>A newton (N): its definition directly uses fundamental units: 1 N =3D=
 1 kg.m.s-2</li>		<li>A dyne (dyn) is defined with the newton: 1 dyn =3D 10=
-5 N</li>		<li>A kilopond (kp) is: 1 kp =3D 9.80665 N</li>	</ul>
	</li></ul>

<p>This definitely requires a more complex model.</p>

<h2>Day 2: <a id=3D"Day2" name=3D"Day2">Handling Prefixes</a></h2>

<p>Let=E2=80=99s start with standard and binary prefixes. These prefixes (<=
code>=E2=80=9CG=E2=80=9D/=E2=80=9DGiga=E2=80=9D</code>, <code>=E2=80=9Ck=E2=
=80=9D/=E2=80=9DKilo=E2=80=9D</code>, <code>=E2=80=9CK=E2=80=9D/=E2=80=9DKi=
bi=E2=80=9D</code>, <code>=E2=80=9Cm=E2=80=9D/=E2=80=9DMilli=E2=80=9D</code=
>, etc.) apply only to non-exponentiated units like the fundamental units a=
nd can be used wherever a fundamental unit is used.</p>

<p>A deci(squared metre) is NOT Valid, however a squared(decimetre) is.</p>

<p>To properly model this, we refine a little bit our model by introducing =
a <code>PrefixedMeasureUnit</code> at the same (lowest) level as the <code>=
FundamentalMeasureUnit</code>.</p>

<p>To unify these two types, we introduce a new <code>AtomicMeasureUnit</co=
de> class that is the base class of <code>FundamentalMeasureUnit</code> and=
 <code>PrefixedMeasureUnit</code>.</p>

<p>The <code>NormalizedMeasureUnit</code> now handles one or more (possibly=
 exponentiated) fundamental units as well as <code>PrefixedMeasureUnit</cod=
e> that are not =E2=80=9Cfundamentals=E2=80=9D. Its name (=E2=80=9Cnormaliz=
ed=E2=80=9D) is no more accurate: we change it to just be a <code>CombinedM=
easureUnit</code> that better reflects what it is.</p>

<p>Since we are dealing with (bad) names, the =E2=80=9C<strong>Basic measur=
e unit</strong>=E2=80=9D does not convey a clear idea of what it is. We ren=
ame the <code>BasicMeasureUnit</code> to be <code>ExponentMeasureUnit</code=
>.</p>

<p>The new class diagram is:</p>

