<p>To conclude this second day, one needs to consider a not-so-edge-case: s=
tandard prefixes are not =E2=80=9Ccomplete=E2=80=9D enough to be safely use=
d. What happens if you want the <code>=E2=80=9CDeci=E2=80=9D</code> (10<sup=
>-1</sup>) of a <code>=E2=80=9CGiga=E2=80=9D</code> (10<sup>9</sup>)? There=
 is no prefix available for 10<sup>8</sup>. Similar question: what is a Kil=
o Yotta (Yotta is the biggest prefix: 10<sup>24</sup>).</p>

<p>Note that you may not ask it directly, but this may happen indirectly in=
 a complex system. We must be able to cope with these cases, even if we=E2=
=80=99ll always try to <strong>eventually</strong> avoid such =E2=80=9Cinte=
rmediate=E2=80=9D prefixes.</p>

<p>The idea is to introduce an =E2=80=9Cadjustment factor=E2=80=9D to our <=
code>PrefixedMeasureUnit</code>. This adjustment is a <code>ExpFactor</code=
> that enables us to handle exponents without relying on floating point typ=
es (and their intrinsic limitations).</p>

