/// Immutable value type that captures 10^&lt;see cref=3D"Exp10"/&gt;.2^&lt=
;see cref=3D"Exp2"/&gt;.
/// &lt;/summary&gt;
public struct ExpFactor : IComparable&lt;ExpFactor&gt;, IEquatable&lt;ExpFa=
ctor&gt;
{
   public static readonly ExpFactor Neutral; // The neutral factor (0,0).


   public readonly int Exp2; // The base 2 exponent.


   public readonly int Exp10; // The base 10 exponent.


   public ExpFactor Power( int p ) =3D&gt; new ExpFactor( Exp2 * p, Exp10 *=
 p );


   public ExpFactor Multiply( ExpFactor x ) =3D&gt; new ExpFactor( Exp2 + x=
.Exp2, Exp10 + x.Exp10 );


   public ExpFactor DivideBy( ExpFactor x ) =3D&gt; new ExpFactor( Exp2 - x=
.Exp2, Exp10 - x.Exp10 );
}

</pre>

<p lang=3D"cs"><strong>Note</strong>: There is a little bit more code in th=
e <code>struct</code> above, we only show here the most relevant aspects.</=
p>

<p>There are two exponents: one in base 2 (since we support binary prefixes=
) and the other in base 10 for metric prefixes.</p>

<p>This allows us to generate =E2=80=9Cintermediate prefixes=E2=80=9D or =
=E2=80=9Cout-of-bound=E2=80=9D prefixes as required and keep the whole syst=
em safe and coherent as long as we find a way to express/show/recognize the=
se beasts.</p>

<p>If an adjustment factor is not the neutral one, it appears in the abbrev=
iation and name of the unit of measure: =E2=80=9C(10^-1)Gm=E2=80=9D/=E2=80=
=9D(10^-1)Gigameter=E2=80=9D will be the =E2=80=9CDecigigametre=E2=80=9D th=
at do not exist. Of course, you will never meet the =E2=80=9C(10^-1)cm=E2=
=80=9D since this is a =E2=80=9Cmm=E2=80=9D/=E2=80=9DMillimetre=E2=80=9D!</=
p>

<p><strong>Note the syntax</strong>: We have deliberately chosen a syntax t=
hat differs from the standard exponent (with the base and the caret ^) so t=
hat these beasts can easily been spotted.</p>

