public void Quantity_operators_override()
{
    var d1 =3D 1.WithUnit( MeasureUnit.Metre );
    var d2 =3D 2.WithUnit( MeasureUnit.Metre );
    var d3 =3D d1 + d2;
    d3.Value.Should().Be( 3.0 );

    var d6 =3D d3 * 2;
    d6.Value.Should().Be( 6.0 );

    var s36 =3D d6 ^ 2;
    s36.Value.Should().Be( 36.0 );
    s36.Unit.Should().Be( MeasureUnit.Metre * MeasureUnit.Metre );

    var sM36 =3D -s36;
    sM36.Value.Should().Be( -36.0 );
    sM36.Unit.Should().Be( MeasureUnit.Metre * MeasureUnit.Metre );

    (s36 - sM36).Value.Should().Be( 72.0 );
    (s36 + sM36).Value.Should().Be( 0.0 );

    var s9 =3D d3 * d3;

    s9.Value.Should().Be( 9.0 );
    s9.Unit.Should().Be( MeasureUnit.Metre * MeasureUnit.Metre );

    var r4 =3D s36 / s9;
    r4.Value.Should().Be( 4 );
    r4.Unit.Should().Be( MeasureUnit.None );

    var s144 =3D r4 * s36;
    s144.Value.Should().Be( 144.0 );
    s144.Unit.Should().Be( MeasureUnit.Metre * MeasureUnit.Metre );

    var v27 =3D d3 ^ 3;
    v27.Value.Should().Be( 27.0 );
    v27.Unit.Should().Be( MeasureUnit.Metre ^ 3 );

    (v27 / s9).Should().Be( d3 );
    ((v27 / s9) =3D=3D d3).Should().BeTrue();
    ((v27 / s9) !=3D d3).Should().BeFalse();
}</pre>

<p>And then I had an=E2=80=A6 issue. Any C#/Java/=E2=80=A6 developer knows =
that whenever you define <code>Equals</code> (here implementing the <code>I=
Equatable&lt;Quantity&gt; interface</code>) on an object, it is expected th=
at you also override the <code>Equals(object)</code> and the <code>GetHashC=
ode()</code> methods.</p>

<p>First, we need to define the equality of quantities:</p>

<ul>
	<li>They must be convertible to each other units.</li>	<li>Their values mu=
st be the same when converted into one (or the other) units.</li></ul>

<p>Thanks to this, <code>=E2=80=9C10 dm=E2=80=9D</code> (decimeter) will be=
 equal to <code>=E2=80=9C1 m=E2=80=9D</code>, <code>=E2=80=9C0.1 hm=E2=80=
=9D</code> (hectometer), <code>=E2=80=9C0.001 km=E2=80=9D</code>, etc.</p>

<p>So far so good, implementing equality implies to convert the other quant=
ity into the unit of this quantity and check the resulting value. Of course=
, if the other quantity cannot be converted, the two quantities are not equ=
al:</p>

