<p>Do you feel comfortable with this? I=E2=80=99m not.</p>

<p>What we have considered so far is that any standard prefix can always be=
en applied to any unit, however this leads to this behavior: defining <code=
>=E2=80=9Cin=E2=80=9D/=E2=80=9DInch=E2=80=9D</code> prevents to define <cod=
e>=E2=80=9Cmin=E2=80=9D/=E2=80=9DMinute=E2=80=9D</code>=E2=80=A6</p>

<p>All standard prefixes should not apply to all units. <code>KiloHour</cod=
e> or <code>MilliMinute</code> are (usually) stupid units. They can always =
be explicitly defined as an <code>AliasMeasureUnit</code> if needed but sho=
uld not be automatically considered.</p>

